Suffix-boundary shapes. Exists because the classifier's suffix test changed from
a bare `ends_with` to sing-box's label-boundary rule, and the previous corpus
could only show the *bare* shape — it had no dot-led entry and no entry that was
a proper label-suffix of another, so "which entry did it pick" was never really
under test.

The three entries overlap on purpose: `xz.example` must miss `z.example` but hit
`example`, and `a.d.example` must prefer `.d.example` over `example` while
`d.example` itself misses the dot-led one entirely. That is the whole rule in
three lines.
